.TH DOH 2
.SH NAME
doh: resolve, resolvetype, Resolver \- DNS over HTTPS
.SH SYNOPSIS
.EX
include "sys.m";
include "keyring.m";
include "tls12.m";
include "https.m";
include "doh.m";
doh := load DOH DOH->PATH;

init:        fn(): string;
gdns:        fn(): ref Resolver;
resolve:     fn(r: ref Resolver, name: string): (list of string, string);
resolvetype: fn(r: ref Resolver, name: string, qtype: int): (list of string, string);
encode:      fn(name: string, qtype, id: int): array of byte;
decode:      fn(msg: array of byte, qtype: int): (list of string, string);

Ta:    con 1;    # A
Taaaa: con 28;   # AAAA
Resolver: adt { url: string; ip: string; };
.EE
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B Doh
resolves hostnames using DNS over HTTPS (RFC 8484), over this tree's
own TLS
.RI ( https (2)/ tls12 (2)) \(em
so the system can turn a name into an address by itself, with no
.I /net/cs
and no host resolver. It is the first thing in the tree that can do
so; every other network client so far has dialled literal IPs or
leaned on the hosted
.I emu 's
own resolver.
.PP
.B Init
must be called once.
.B Resolve
returns
.IR name 's
A records as dotted-decimal strings;
.B resolvetype
takes an explicit
.B Ta
or
.B Taaaa
(A gives dotted IPv4, AAAA colon-hex IPv6).
.I R
is the resolver to use, or nil for
.B gdns
(Google Public DNS,
.B dns.google
at
.BR 8.8.8.8 ).
.PP
The bootstrap is the essential trick: a
.B Resolver
carries both the DoH
.B url
(whose host the certificate is verified against) and a literal
.B ip
to dial it at. Reaching the resolver therefore needs no prior DNS \(em
.IR https (2)'s
.B requestto
dials the known IP while still validating the resolver's real
hostname \(em which is what lets DNS bootstrap at all.
.PP
.B Encode
and
.B decode
are the DNS wire-format halves (RFC 1035), exposed for offline testing
against captured bytes:
.B encode
builds a query message,
.B decode
pulls the addresses of the requested type from a response (following
the message-compression pointers real answers use, and reporting a
non-zero RCODE \(em e.g. name-does-not-exist \(em as an error).
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/lib/doh.b
.br
.B /module/doh.m
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR dns (1),
.IR dohtest (1),
.IR https (2),
.IR tls12 (2)
.SH BUGS
.B Live resolution does not work yet, and the reason is not in this
.BR module .
Every current public DoH resolver
.RB ( dns.google ,
Cloudflare, Quad9, and the rest) presents an ECDSA (secp256r1)
certificate, and several require P-256 ECDHE key exchange;
.IR tls12 (2)
verifies RSA certificate chains over X25519 only, so the TLS
handshake to a real resolver fails before any DNS is exchanged. The
wire format here is complete and verified offline
.RI ( dohtest (1)),
and live resolution will work unchanged once ECDSA/P-256 support lands
in the TLS stack \(em that is the gating work, tracked with the rest
of the TLS modernization in
.I doc/ssl-modernization-plan.md .
.PP
Only A and AAAA records are handled; there is no caching, no fallback
across multiple resolvers, and no integration into
.IR dial (2)'s
own name resolution (a caller must invoke
.B doh
explicitly). The message id is always 0, as RFC 8484 recommends for
DoH.
